00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:29.167
How the grocery platform ships and runs code, containers, health checks, deployment strategies, autoscaling and infrastructure-as-code.

9
00:00:29.447 --> 00:00:40.967
The container-limits lab uses real Linux cgroups (what Docker or Kubernetes use underneath); the rest run real HTTP instances behind a real load balancer.

10
00:00:41.247 --> 00:00:43.188
L1: deployment strategies.

11
00:00:43.468 --> 00:01:04.759
Shipping a bad build (40% errors) four ways: recreate broke 45.9% of requests, rolling 26.0%, blue-green 0% (a smoke test caught it before the flip), and canary 1.5% (auto-rolled back after ~0.9 s, blast radius = the canary).

12
00:01:06.109 --> 00:01:07.538
L2: health probes.

13
00:01:07.818 --> 00:01:19.082
With no probes, a slow-starting instance broke 40.6% of requests, a DB-less instance 22.7%, a hung instance 11.9%.

14
00:01:19.362 --> 00:01:27.661
Readiness and liveness probes cut those to 0.4% / 1.0% / 0.4%.

15
00:01:27.941 --> 00:01:29.562
L3: autoscaling.

16
00:01:29.842 --> 00:01:39.549
A reactive loop with no stabilization made 827 scaling actions; a scale-down window cut that to 101 with fewer breaches.

17
00:01:39.829 --> 00:01:47.082
Ignoring JVM warm-up under-scaled; pre-scaling a known peak cut SLO breaches from 16 to 1.

18
00:01:48.432 --> 00:01:51.163
L4: container limits (real cgroups).

19
00:01:51.443 --> 00:01:58.824
The JVM's heap tracked the cgroup memory limit (1498, then 185 MB).

20
00:01:59.104 --> 00:02:08.427
Exceeding it with -Xmx too high got a kernel SIGKILL (exit 137); MaxRAMPercentage failed cleanly (exit 3).

21
00:02:08.707 --> 00:02:15.320
A 0.5-CPU quota throttled the process 182 times and doubled the runtime.

22
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:16.581
Reading time:

23
00:02:16.861 --> 00:02:18.824
120 min + code.

24
00:02:20.174 --> 00:02:21.027
Part 1.

25
00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:23.227
Introduction and Why It Matters.

26
00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.952
A story: the deploy that took the store down.

27
00:02:27.232 --> 00:02:31.883
A routine Tuesday deploy of checkout-service took checkout down for eight minutes.

28
00:02:32.163 --> 00:02:37.027
The post-incident review found five separate gaps, each a section of this chapter:

29
00:02:37.307 --> 00:02:39.483
The deploy strategy was recreate.

30
00:02:39.763 --> 00:02:52.563
The CI job stopped all four instances and started the new version, so there was a window with nothing serving (503s), and then the new build, which had a config bug, served errors to everyone at once.

31
00:02:52.843 --> 00:02:56.917
L1 measures this: recreate broke 46% of requests.

32
00:02:58.267 --> 00:02:59.761
There were no readiness probes.

33
00:03:00.041 --> 00:03:12.051
The load balancer routed traffic to new instances the instant the process started, before the JVM had warmed up and the connection pool had filled, so the first requests to each instance failed.

34
00:03:12.331 --> 00:03:17.857
L2: a slow-starting instance with no readiness probe broke 40% of requests.

35
00:03:18.137 --> 00:03:20.163
A hung instance kept taking traffic.

36
00:03:20.443 --> 00:03:28.529
One instance deadlocked; with no liveness probe, nothing restarted it, and it black-holed its share of requests until a human noticed.

37
00:03:28.809 --> 00:03:29.619
L2:

38
00:03:29.899 --> 00:03:35.254
11.9% failures, versus 0.4% with a liveness probe.

39
00:03:36.604 --> 00:03:37.991
Autoscaling thrashed.

40
00:03:38.271 --> 00:03:45.503
During the incident's traffic spike, the autoscaler with no stabilization scaled up and down every few seconds (L3:

41
00:03:45.783 --> 00:03:52.439
827 actions), and new replicas weren't counted as still warming, so it was always a step behind.

42
00:03:52.719 --> 00:03:54.489
The rollback was manual and slow.

43
00:03:54.769 --> 00:04:00.657
Nobody could flip back to the old version quickly, because the old version was already gone (recreate).

44
00:04:00.937 --> 00:04:04.415
Blue-green or canary would have made rollback instant (L1).

45
00:04:05.765 --> 00:04:20.485
The rebuild: immutable containers, canary deploys with automatic rollback, readiness or liveness or startup probes, stabilized autoscaling that models warm-up, and infrastructure as code so environments are reproducible.

46
00:04:21.835 --> 00:04:22.517
Quote:

47
00:04:22.797 --> 00:04:24.461
The thesis of this chapter.

48
00:04:24.741 --> 00:04:27.493
Deployment is where good design meets reality.

49
00:04:27.773 --> 00:04:42.472
The goal is to ship frequently and safely: every change goes out to a small blast radius first, behind health checks that gate traffic and restarts, on immutable infrastructure defined as code so it's reproducible and reversible.

50
00:04:42.752 --> 00:04:46.571
A deploy you can't roll back in seconds is a deploy you shouldn't make.

51
00:04:46.851 --> 00:04:57.944
And the platform (containers, limits, autoscaling) must match the runtime (the JVM's warm-up and cgroup awareness, Ch 25), or it fights the application.

52
