111
00:07:21.904 --> 00:07:22.779
Part 2.

112
00:07:23.059 --> 00:07:24.957
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

113
00:07:26.207 --> 00:07:27.125
Intuition.

114
00:07:27.405 --> 00:07:29.453
A container is a shipping container.

115
00:07:29.733 --> 00:07:41.615
The same sealed box (image) moves unchanged from the developer's laptop to production; what's inside is isolated and the box declares how much space and power it needs (limits).

116
00:07:41.895 --> 00:07:44.157
Works on my machine stops being a defense.

117
00:07:44.437 --> 00:07:46.954
Health probes are a bouncer and a paramedic.

118
00:07:47.234 --> 00:07:53.613
Readiness is the bouncer at the door: an instance that isn't ready to serve doesn't get customers (traffic).

119
00:07:53.893 --> 00:07:59.717
Liveness is the paramedic: an instance that has collapsed (hung) gets carried out and replaced.

120
00:07:59.997 --> 00:08:04.605
Confusing the two, restarting on readiness failures, causes restart storms.

121
00:08:05.955 --> 00:08:08.472
Deployment strategy is how you taste-test.

122
00:08:08.752 --> 00:08:13.104
Recreate throws out the old dish and serves the new one to everyone at once.

123
00:08:13.384 --> 00:08:18.227
Canary gives a spoonful to a few diners and watches their faces before serving the room.

124
00:08:18.507 --> 00:08:24.160
Blue-green cooks the whole new batch, tastes it in the kitchen, and swaps the trays only if it's good.

125
00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.317
Autoscaling is a thermostat.

126
00:08:26.597 --> 00:08:30.352
It adds capacity when the room heats up and removes it when it cools.

127
00:08:30.632 --> 00:08:38.739
A twitchy thermostat cycles the heating on and off constantly (thrashing); a good one waits a bit before reacting (stabilization).

128
00:08:40.089 --> 00:08:41.027
Containers.

129
00:08:41.307 --> 00:08:54.641
A container is a process (or group) isolated by Linux namespaces (its own view of the filesystem, network, PIDs) and limited by cgroups (how much CPU, memory, I or O it may use).

130
00:08:54.921 --> 00:09:02.174
It is not a VM, there's no guest kernel; containers share the host kernel, which is why they're light and fast to start.

131
00:09:02.454 --> 00:09:06.443
Images are immutable, layered filesystems (OCI format).

132
00:09:06.723 --> 00:09:13.507
You build once and run the identical bytes everywhere, addressed by content digest (not a mutable latest tag).

133
00:09:13.787 --> 00:09:17.542
Layers are cached and shared, so a small change ships a small delta.

134
00:09:18.892 --> 00:09:34.892
Build well: multi-stage builds (compile in a fat image, copy the artifact into a slim runtime image), a minimal base (distroless or Alpine or eclipse-temurin:-jre), non-root user, no secrets in layers, and pinned base digests.

135
00:09:35.172 --> 00:09:40.612
A JVM image should carry a JRE, not a JDK. cgroups are the limits.

136
00:09:40.892 --> 00:10:00.881
L4 shows the JVM reading its cgroup memory limit: heap tracked the limit (1498 MB host, then 185 MB in a 256 MB cgroup at MaxRAMPercentage=75), and a CPU quota changed availableProcessors.

137
00:10:01.161 --> 00:10:04.639
This is exactly what Kubernetes resources.limits set.

138
00:10:05.989 --> 00:10:07.909
Orchestration and Kubernetes.

139
00:10:08.189 --> 00:10:12.050
At scale you don't run containers by hand; an orchestrator does.

140
00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:14.677
Kubernetes is the de-facto standard.

141
00:10:14.957 --> 00:10:16.087
The core objects:

142
00:10:17.437 --> 00:10:18.803
Here is a quick table:

143
00:10:19.083 --> 00:10:20.555
Object, and What it is.

144
00:10:20.835 --> 00:10:21.603
Pod:

145
00:10:21.883 --> 00:10:27.813
One or more co-located containers, the smallest deployable unit (usually one app container).

146
00:10:28.093 --> 00:10:29.032
Deployment:

147
00:10:29.312 --> 00:10:37.547
Declares N replicas of a Pod template; the controller reconciles reality to that desired state, and manages rolling updates.

148
00:10:37.827 --> 00:10:38.659
Service:

149
00:10:38.939 --> 00:10:46.405
A stable virtual IP or DNS name load-balancing across a Deployment's ready Pods (Ch 08).

150
00:10:46.685 --> 00:10:48.008
Ingress or Gateway:

151
00:10:48.288 --> 00:10:52.661
External HTTP routing into Services (Ch 08).

152
00:10:52.941 --> 00:10:54.477
ConfigMap or Secret:

153
00:10:54.757 --> 00:11:00.389
Configuration and secrets injected as env vars or files (§2.7).

154
00:11:00.669 --> 00:11:02.504
HorizontalPodAutoscaler:

155
00:11:02.784 --> 00:11:06.837
Scales replica count on metrics (§2.6, L3).

156
00:11:08.187 --> 00:11:09.275
StatefulSet:

157
00:11:09.555 --> 00:11:13.843
Pods with stable identity and storage (databases, Ch 11).

158
00:11:15.193 --> 00:11:30.297
The key idea is declarative desired state and reconciliation: you say I want 4 ready replicas of this image, and controllers continuously make it so, replacing crashed Pods, rolling out new versions, rescheduling off dead nodes.

159
00:11:30.577 --> 00:11:32.455
You manage intent, not steps.

160
00:11:33.805 --> 00:11:34.850
Health probes.

161
00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.349
Three distinct questions, three probes:

162
00:11:38.699 --> 00:11:40.064
Here is a quick table:

163
00:11:40.344 --> 00:11:43.523
Probe, and Question, and On failure, and Endpoint checks.

164
00:11:43.803 --> 00:11:44.656
Readiness.

165
00:11:44.936 --> 00:11:45.811
Question:

166
00:11:46.091 --> 00:11:47.413
Can I serve right now?

167
00:11:47.693 --> 00:11:48.589
On failure:

168
00:11:48.869 --> 00:11:51.408
Remove from the load balancer (stop routing).

169
00:11:51.688 --> 00:11:52.776
Endpoint checks:

170
00:11:53.056 --> 00:11:57.643
Dependencies ready (DB pool filled, caches warm, downstream reachable).

171
00:11:57.923 --> 00:11:58.797
Liveness.

172
00:11:59.077 --> 00:11:59.952
Question:

173
00:12:00.232 --> 00:12:01.939
Am I alive (not deadlocked)?

174
00:12:02.219 --> 00:12:03.115
On failure:

175
00:12:03.395 --> 00:12:04.739
Restart the container.

176
00:12:05.019 --> 00:12:06.107
Endpoint checks:

177
00:12:06.387 --> 00:12:09.352
Process responsive; a simple am I looping check.

178
00:12:09.632 --> 00:12:10.443
Startup.

179
00:12:10.723 --> 00:12:11.597
Question:

180
00:12:11.877 --> 00:12:13.157
Have I finished booting?

181
00:12:13.437 --> 00:12:14.333
On failure:

182
00:12:14.613 --> 00:12:16.384
Hold off liveness until done.

183
00:12:16.664 --> 00:12:17.752
Endpoint checks:

184
00:12:18.032 --> 00:12:21.723
Slow initialization; protects a slow boot from liveness kills.

185
00:12:23.073 --> 00:12:26.038
L2 measured all three against real instances:

186
00:12:27.388 --> 00:12:28.753
Here is a quick table:

187
00:12:29.033 --> 00:12:31.423
Scenario, and No probes, and With probes.

188
00:12:31.703 --> 00:12:34.647
Slow-starting instance (routed to before ready).

189
00:12:34.927 --> 00:12:35.887
No probes:

190
00:12:36.167 --> 00:12:38.023
40.6% errors.

191
00:12:38.303 --> 00:12:39.220
With probes:

192
00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.380
0.4% (readiness holds it out).

193
00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:44.964
Instance loses its DB mid-flight.

194
00:12:45.244 --> 00:12:46.204
No probes:

195
00:12:46.484 --> 00:12:48.468
22.7% errors.

196
00:12:48.748 --> 00:12:49.665
With probes:

197
00:12:49.945 --> 00:12:52.655
1.0% (readiness drains it).

198
00:12:52.935 --> 00:12:54.492
Instance hangs (deadlock).

199
00:12:54.772 --> 00:12:55.732
No probes:

200
00:12:56.012 --> 00:12:57.889
11.9% errors.

201
00:12:58.169 --> 00:12:59.087
With probes:

202
00:12:59.367 --> 00:13:02.951
0.4% (liveness restarts it, 1 times ).

203
00:13:04.301 --> 00:13:05.474
The critical rules:

204
00:13:05.754 --> 00:13:08.805
Readiness gates routing; liveness gates restarts.

205
00:13:09.085 --> 00:13:14.269
Never restart on a readiness failure, a dependency blip would restart-storm your whole fleet.

206
00:13:14.549 --> 00:13:20.885
Never route on liveness alone, a live-but-not-ready instance (still warming) gets traffic it can't serve.

207
00:13:21.165 --> 00:13:23.938
A liveness probe must be cheap and self-contained.

208
00:13:24.218 --> 00:13:32.773
If it checks the database, then a database outage makes Kubernetes restart every pod (turning a dependency outage into a total outage).

209
00:13:33.053 --> 00:13:36.210
Check is this process responsive, nothing downstream.

210
00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:47.053
A startup probe protects slow-booting apps (the JVM, Ch 25) from an aggressive liveness timeout that would kill them mid-boot in a crash loop.

211
00:13:48.403 --> 00:13:49.769
Deployment strategies.

212
00:13:50.049 --> 00:13:51.414
Here is a quick table:

213
00:13:51.694 --> 00:13:56.771
Strategy, and How, and Downtime, and Blast radius of a bad build, and Rollback.

214
00:13:57.051 --> 00:13:58.054
Recreate.

215
00:13:58.334 --> 00:13:58.995
How:

216
00:13:59.275 --> 00:14:01.131
