217
00:14:01.411 --> 00:14:02.307
Downtime:

218
00:14:02.587 --> 00:14:03.313
Yes.

219
00:14:03.593 --> 00:14:05.427
Blast radius of a bad build:

220
00:14:05.707 --> 00:14:07.542
100% (everyone).

221
00:14:07.822 --> 00:14:08.718
Rollback:

222
00:14:08.998 --> 00:14:10.385
Redeploy old (slow).

223
00:14:10.665 --> 00:14:11.454
Rolling.

224
00:14:11.734 --> 00:14:12.395
How:

225
00:14:12.675 --> 00:14:14.809
Replace instances a few at a time.

226
00:14:15.089 --> 00:14:15.985
Downtime:

227
00:14:16.265 --> 00:14:16.947
No.

228
00:14:17.227 --> 00:14:19.062
Blast radius of a bad build:

229
00:14:19.342 --> 00:14:21.838
Ramps to 100% as it completes.

230
00:14:22.118 --> 00:14:23.014
Rollback:

231
00:14:23.294 --> 00:14:25.043
Roll forward or back (minutes).

232
00:14:25.323 --> 00:14:26.262
Blue-green.

233
00:14:26.542 --> 00:14:27.203
How:

234
00:14:27.483 --> 00:14:30.086
Build a full new pool, test it, flip traffic.

235
00:14:30.366 --> 00:14:31.262
Downtime:

236
00:14:31.542 --> 00:14:32.225
No.

237
00:14:32.505 --> 00:14:34.339
Blast radius of a bad build:

238
00:14:34.619 --> 00:14:38.459
0% if tested first; else 100% at flip.

239
00:14:38.739 --> 00:14:39.635
Rollback:

240
00:14:39.915 --> 00:14:41.238
Instant (flip back).

241
00:14:41.518 --> 00:14:42.329
Canary.

242
00:14:42.609 --> 00:14:43.270
How:

243
00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:47.582
Send a small % to the new version, watch, promote or roll back.

244
00:14:47.862 --> 00:14:48.758
Downtime:

245
00:14:49.038 --> 00:14:49.721
No.

246
00:14:50.001 --> 00:14:51.835
Blast radius of a bad build:

247
00:14:52.115 --> 00:14:53.566
The canary % only.

248
00:14:53.846 --> 00:14:54.742
Rollback:

249
00:14:55.022 --> 00:14:56.601
Instant (remove canary).

250
00:14:57.951 --> 00:15:03.604
L1 shipped a bad build (40% errors) each way and measured user-visible failures:

251
00:15:03.884 --> 00:15:04.972
Recreate:

252
00:15:05.252 --> 00:15:10.415
45.9%, a downtime window plus full exposure to the bad build.

253
00:15:10.695 --> 00:15:11.484
Rolling:

254
00:15:11.764 --> 00:15:21.705
26.0%, no downtime, but errors climb toward 100% as the roll completes; you're exposing everyone, just gradually.

255
00:15:21.985 --> 00:15:22.903
Blue-green:

256
00:15:23.183 --> 00:15:32.079
0%, a smoke test on the green pool caught the bad build before the flip, so it never went live; if it had passed, a flip-back is instant.

257
00:15:32.359 --> 00:15:33.127
Canary:

258
00:15:33.407 --> 00:15:46.207
1.5%, only the canary's traffic saw errors; the controller observed a 37% error rate ( 5% SLO) and auto-rolled back after ~0.9 s.

259
00:15:47.557 --> 00:15:58.266
Progressive delivery combines canary with automated analysis (error rate, latency, business metrics) and automatic promotion or rollback (Argo Rollouts, Flagger).

260
00:15:58.546 --> 00:16:07.719
Feature flags decouple deploy from release: ship the code dark, then turn it on for a cohort, a separate, faster rollback lever than redeploying.

261
00:16:09.069 --> 00:16:10.072
Autoscaling.

262
00:16:10.352 --> 00:16:13.744
Horizontal (HPA): add or remove replicas.

263
00:16:14.024 --> 00:16:19.165
The formula: desired = ceil(current times currentMetric or targetMetric).

264
00:16:19.445 --> 00:16:29.131
Scale on the right metric, RED or utilization or a custom metric (queue depth, in-flight requests), not just CPU (Ch 24).

265
00:16:29.411 --> 00:16:32.973
Vertical (VPA): resize a pod's requests or limits.

266
00:16:33.253 --> 00:16:37.989
Useful for right-sizing, awkward for live traffic (usually needs a restart).

267
00:16:38.269 --> 00:16:44.221
Cluster autoscaler: add or remove nodes when pods can't be scheduled or nodes are idle.

268
00:16:44.501 --> 00:16:50.411
L3 ran an HPA-style control loop over a day's load (diurnal + a flash-sale spike):

269
00:16:51.761 --> 00:16:53.126
Here is a quick table:

270
00:16:53.406 --> 00:16:58.611
Configuration, and SLO breaches, and Scaling actions, and Avg replicas.

271
00:16:58.891 --> 00:17:00.769
Reactive, no stabilization.

272
00:17:01.049 --> 00:17:02.329
SLO breaches:

273
00:17:02.609 --> 00:17:03.590
47.

274
00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:05.065
Scaling actions:

275
00:17:05.345 --> 00:17:06.859
827.

276
00:17:07.139 --> 00:17:08.547
Avg replicas:

277
00:17:08.827 --> 00:17:12.475
8.2. + scale-down stabilization (30 s).

278
00:17:12.755 --> 00:17:14.035
SLO breaches:

279
00:17:14.315 --> 00:17:15.254
16.

280
00:17:15.534 --> 00:17:16.729
Scaling actions:

281
00:17:17.009 --> 00:17:18.118
101.

282
00:17:18.398 --> 00:17:19.806
Avg replicas:

283
00:17:20.086 --> 00:17:21.153
8.9.

284
00:17:21.433 --> 00:17:23.054
Controller ignores warm-up.

285
00:17:23.334 --> 00:17:24.614
SLO breaches:

286
00:17:24.894 --> 00:17:25.705
18.

287
00:17:25.985 --> 00:17:27.179
Scaling actions:

288
00:17:27.459 --> 00:17:28.953
126.

289
00:17:29.233 --> 00:17:30.641
Avg replicas:

290
00:17:30.921 --> 00:17:33.993
8.4. + predictive pre-scale for the peak.

291
00:17:34.273 --> 00:17:35.553
SLO breaches:

292
00:17:35.833 --> 00:17:36.473
1.

293
00:17:36.753 --> 00:17:37.947
Scaling actions:

294
00:17:38.227 --> 00:17:39.657
131.

295
00:17:39.937 --> 00:17:41.345
Avg replicas:

296
00:17:41.625 --> 00:17:42.883
11.6.

297
00:17:44.233 --> 00:17:47.391
Stabilization (a scale-down window) stops thrashing:

298
00:17:47.671 --> 00:17:50.807
827, then 101 actions.

299
00:17:51.087 --> 00:17:57.913
Scaling up should be fast, scaling down slow, so a brief dip doesn't tear down capacity you'll need again in seconds.

300
00:17:58.193 --> 00:18:05.788
Warm-up matters: a new replica isn't at full capacity for ~15 s (the JVM, Ch 25).

301
00:18:06.068 --> 00:18:12.297
A controller that counts cold replicas as full under-scales and breaches more (18 versus 16).

302
00:18:12.577 --> 00:18:15.265
Model warm-up, or scale on a leading signal.

303
00:18:16.615 --> 00:18:30.077
Reactive scaling is always a step behind a fast spike; for known peaks (a sale), pre-scale on a schedule or a leading indicator, L3 cut breaches from 16 to 1 (at the cost of running more replicas).

304
00:18:30.357 --> 00:18:35.519
Combine with load shedding (Ch 18) for the seconds before capacity arrives.

305
00:18:36.869 --> 00:18:39.131
Config and secrets (12-factor).

306
00:18:39.411 --> 00:18:41.800
Config in the environment, not the image.

307
00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:48.651
The same image runs in dev or staging or prod; only injected config differs (12-factor).

308
00:18:48.931 --> 00:18:59.768
ConfigMaps (or env vars) for non-secret config; a secrets manager (Ch 23) for secrets, mounted at runtime, never baked into layers or committed.

309
00:19:00.048 --> 00:19:06.128
Immutability: a released image is never modified; a change is a new image with a new digest.

310
00:19:06.408 --> 00:19:09.672
This makes deploys reproducible and rollbacks exact.

311
00:19:09.952 --> 00:19:20.960
Config changes are deploys too: a bad ConfigMap can break prod as surely as bad code, so version and roll it out with the same care (canary a config change where you can).

312
00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:36.091
Externalize state: containers are ephemeral and disposable (12-factor processes are stateless); state lives in databases, caches and object storage (Ch 21), not on the container's disk.

313
00:19:37.441 --> 00:19:38.636
CI or CD.

314
00:19:38.916 --> 00:19:42.009
A pipeline turns a commit into a safe production change:

315
00:19:42.289 --> 00:19:47.089
Build the image (reproducible, from a pinned base), tag by commit or digest.

316
00:19:47.369 --> 00:19:54.452
Test: unit, integration, contract (Ch 29), and a small load or perf gate (Ch 25).

317
00:19:54.732 --> 00:19:55.479
Scan:

318
00:19:55.759 --> 00:20:02.159
SAST, dependency or SBOM and image vulnerability scans, secret scanning (Ch 23).

319
00:20:02.439 --> 00:20:05.639
Deploy to staging, run smoke or e2e tests.

320
00:20:05.919 --> 00:20:10.825
Canary to production with automated analysis and rollback (§2.5).

321
00:20:11.105 --> 00:20:15.863
Promote to 100%, or roll back automatically on an SLO breach.

322
00:20:17.213 --> 00:20:26.365
Continuous Delivery (every green build is deployable, humans approve the release) versus Continuous Deployment (every green build is released).

323
00:20:26.645 --> 00:20:28.031
Pick per service risk.

324
00:20:28.311 --> 00:20:35.949
GitOps: the desired cluster state lives in Git; a controller (Argo CD, Flux) reconciles the cluster to it.

325
00:20:36.229 --> 00:20:41.370
Deploys are pull requests; rollback is a git revert; the repo is an audit log.

326
00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:50.229
DORA metrics measure the pipeline: deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change-failure rate, time to restore.

327
00:20:50.509 --> 00:20:58.061
Elite teams deploy often and fail rarely, the two go together, because small frequent changes are safer than big rare ones.

328
00:20:59.411 --> 00:21:00.819
Infrastructure as code.

329
00:21:01.099 --> 00:21:17.483
Declarative IaC (Terraform, Pulumi, CloudFormation) defines cloud resources, networks, clusters, databases, IAM, as versioned code. plan shows the diff before apply; state tracks reality; the same code builds identical environments.

330
00:21:17.763 --> 00:21:24.675
Immutable infrastructure: don't mutate running servers (no SSH-and-fix); rebuild from code and replace.

331
00:21:24.955 --> 00:21:29.073
This kills configuration drift and the one server nobody dares touch.

332
00:21:30.423 --> 00:21:40.684
Modules and environments: reusable modules, per-environment variables, and a remote, locked state backend so a team can collaborate without clobbering each other.

333
00:21:40.964 --> 00:21:49.049
Policy as code (OPA or Sentinel) enforces guardrails (no public S3 buckets, required tags) in the pipeline.

334
00:21:50.399 --> 00:21:52.426
Resource limits and the JVM.

335
00:21:52.706 --> 00:21:58.679
This is where infrastructure and the runtime meet, and where L4 (real cgroups) makes it concrete:

336
00:21:58.959 --> 00:22:08.751
Requests versus limits: requests are what the scheduler reserves (and bases bin-packing on); limits are hard caps the kernel enforces via cgroups.

337
00:22:10.101 --> 00:22:12.704
Memory: the limit is enforced by the kernel.

338
00:22:12.984 --> 00:22:22.093
Exceed it and the process is OOM-killed (SIGKILL, exit 137), no exception, no stack trace, looks like a mysterious crash.

339
00:22:22.373 --> 00:22:37.968
L4: -Xmx400m in a 256 MB cgroup, then exit 137; MaxRAMPercentage=75 inside the limit, then a clean Java OOM (exit 3) you can alert on.

340
00:22:38.248 --> 00:22:45.245
Set the heap as a percentage of the limit and leave headroom for non-heap memory (metaspace, threads, direct buffers).

341
00:22:46.595 --> 00:22:54.937
CPU: a CPU limit is a cgroup quota; exceeding it means the kernel throttles the process (pauses it until the next period).

342
00:22:55.217 --> 00:23:10.577
L4: a 0.5-CPU quota throttled the workload 182 times and doubled its runtime, and availableProcessors reported the quota (rounded up), which sizes GC threads and pools (Ch 25).

343
00:23:10.857 --> 00:23:21.118
Many teams set CPU requests but avoid hard CPU limits on latency-sensitive services, because throttling mid-request spikes the p99.

344
00:23:21.398 --> 00:23:36.075
The lesson: the container's limits and the JVM's flags must agree, or the platform fights the app, the §1.1-class of it crashes and we don't know why (exit 137) or it's randomly slow (throttling).

345
00:23:37.425 --> 00:23:38.919
Multi-region and edge.

346
00:23:39.199 --> 00:23:49.332
Why multi-region: disaster recovery (Ch 27), latency (serve users from a nearby region), and data residency (Ch 36).

347
00:23:49.612 --> 00:24:03.244
Topologies: active-passive (a standby region, simpler, wasted capacity), active-active (both serve, harder data consistency), or regional isolation (each region self-contained for its users).

348
00:24:03.524 --> 00:24:17.839
The hard part is data, not compute (Ch 10, Ch 11): cross-region replication lag, consistency, and failover of the database are the real problems; stateless services scale to any region easily.

349
00:24:19.189 --> 00:24:20.298
Traffic routing:

350
00:24:20.578 --> 00:24:31.330
GeoDNS or anycast or a global load balancer routes users to the nearest healthy region, with health-based failover (Ch 08, Ch 27).

351
