589
00:43:53.765 --> 00:43:56.090
On screen: === A.

590
00:43:56.370 --> 00:44:07.229
JVM heap sizing follows the cgroup memory limit (-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75) ===. host (no cgroup):

591
00:44:07.509 --> 00:44:18.090
JVM sees: availableProcessors=2, maxMemory=1498 MB. cgroup mem 256 MB:

592
00:44:18.370 --> 00:44:27.693
JVM sees: availableProcessors=2, maxMemory=185 MB. cgroup mem 512 MB:

593
00:44:27.973 --> 00:44:37.893
JVM sees: availableProcessors=2, maxMemory=371 MB. cgroup mem 1024 MB:

594
00:44:38.173 --> 00:44:45.917
JVM sees: availableProcessors=2, maxMemory=742 MB. = B.

595
00:44:46.197 --> 00:45:05.333
Memory limit exceeded, two very different outcomes =. -Xmx400m in a 256 MB cgroup: last log 'allocated 180 MB (live)', exit 137 (137 =. kernel SIGKILL, OOM-killer; no Java exception).

596
00:45:05.613 --> 00:45:15.575
MaxRAMPercentage=75 in 256 MB: last log 'Terminating due to. java.lang.OutOfMemoryError:

597
00:45:15.855 --> 00:45:21.679
Java heap space', exit 3 (3 = clean JVM OOM you can. alert on).

598
00:45:21.959 --> 00:45:23.474
The rest is in the chapter.

599
00:45:26.324 --> 00:45:27.177
Reading it:

600
00:45:27.457 --> 00:45:50.732
A, heap follows the limit: with -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75, the JVM's max heap tracked the cgroup memory limit (host 1498 MB, then 185 or 371 or 742 MB in 256 or 512 or 1024 MB cgroups).

601
00:45:51.012 --> 00:45:56.025
This is why you size the heap as a percentage, not a fixed -Xmx, in a container.

602
00:45:57.375 --> 00:46:17.706
B, the two ways to hit the limit: -Xmx400m in a 256 MB cgroup makes the JVM believe it has 400 MB, so it allocates past 256 MB and the kernel OOM-killer SIGKILLs it, exit 137, with no Java exception (the mysterious crash).

603
00:46:17.986 --> 00:46:28.525
MaxRAMPercentage=75 keeps the JVM inside the limit, so it throws a clean Java OOM (exit 3) you can catch and alert on.

604
00:46:28.805 --> 00:46:32.218
Always size the heap under the limit and leave off-heap headroom.

605
00:46:33.568 --> 00:46:57.419
C, CPU throttling: the same single-threaded work took 7.6 s at a 2-core quota and 15.8 s at 0.5 cores, throttled 182 times, the kernel pauses the process each 100 ms period once it spends its quota. availableProcessors reflected the quota (rounded up), which sizes GC threads and pools (Ch 25).

606
00:46:57.699 --> 00:47:03.416
This is why hard CPU limits spike the p99 of latency-sensitive services.

