692
00:50:45.585 --> 00:50:48.123
Heap or off-heap memory limit (L4).

693
00:50:48.403 --> 00:50:49.406
Mitigation:

694
00:50:49.686 --> 00:50:52.374
MaxRAMPercentage, off-heap headroom.

695
00:50:52.654 --> 00:50:54.553
Random p99 spikes.

696
00:50:54.833 --> 00:50:55.643
Symptom:

697
00:50:55.923 --> 00:50:57.843
Latency unrelated to load.

698
00:50:58.123 --> 00:50:58.891
Cause:

699
00:50:59.171 --> 00:51:01.710
CPU hard-limit throttling (L4).

700
00:51:01.990 --> 00:51:02.993
Mitigation:

701
00:51:03.273 --> 00:51:06.259
Avoid hard CPU limits for latency services.

702
00:51:06.539 --> 00:51:07.585
Config drift.

703
00:51:07.865 --> 00:51:08.675
Symptom:

704
00:51:08.955 --> 00:51:10.833
Works in staging, not prod.

705
00:51:11.113 --> 00:51:11.881
Cause:

706
00:51:12.161 --> 00:51:13.953
Click-ops, mutable servers.

707
00:51:14.233 --> 00:51:15.235
Mitigation:

708
00:51:15.515 --> 00:51:18.118
IaC + immutable infra + GitOps.

709
00:51:19.468 --> 00:51:20.492
Can't roll back.

710
00:51:20.772 --> 00:51:21.583
Symptom:

711
00:51:21.863 --> 00:51:23.783
Long outage during a bad release.

712
00:51:24.063 --> 00:51:24.831
Cause:

713
00:51:25.111 --> 00:51:26.967
Old version gone (recreate).

714
00:51:27.247 --> 00:51:28.249
Mitigation:

715
00:51:28.529 --> 00:51:31.793
Keep old version (blue-green or canary), feature flags.

716
00:51:33.143 --> 00:51:35.383
Observability (Ch 24).

717
00:51:35.663 --> 00:51:43.919
Deploy as an event: annotate dashboards with deploys so a metric change lines up with a release; most incidents follow a change.

718
00:51:44.199 --> 00:51:50.770
Canary analysis metrics: error rate, latency, and business KPIs for the canary versus baseline.

719
00:51:51.050 --> 00:52:01.631
Probe and restart signals: readiness or liveness failure counts and restart counts per pod, a restart spike usually means a bad liveness config, not a bad app.

720
00:52:01.911 --> 00:52:03.170
Scaling decisions:

721
00:52:03.450 --> 00:52:13.989
HPA target versus actual, replica count, scale events; OOM-kills (exit 137) and CPU throttling (cgroup nrthrottled).

722
00:52:15.339 --> 00:52:23.083
DORA metrics: deploy frequency, lead time, change-failure rate, MTTR, the health of the delivery system itself.

723
00:52:24.433 --> 00:52:25.371
Security.

724
00:52:25.651 --> 00:52:38.110
Supply chain: pin base images by digest, scan images and dependencies (SBOM), sign images (Sigstore or cosign) and verify signatures at admission (Ch 23).

725
00:52:38.390 --> 00:52:42.209
A compromised base image or dependency is a production breach.

726
00:52:42.489 --> 00:52:54.542
Least privilege at runtime: non-root containers, read-only root filesystem, dropped capabilities, no host mounts; network policies default-deny (Ch 23 zero trust).

727
00:52:54.822 --> 00:53:02.225
Secrets come from a manager at runtime, never in images, env dumps or logs; rotate them (Ch 23).

728
00:53:03.575 --> 00:53:05.132
The pipeline is production:

729
00:53:05.412 --> 00:53:16.377
CI or CD has deploy credentials, so protect it like prod, signed commits, protected branches, least-privilege runners, and an audit trail (GitOps gives one).

730
00:53:17.727 --> 00:53:19.391
Performance and tuning knobs.

731
00:53:19.671 --> 00:53:21.037
Here is a quick table:

732
00:53:21.317 --> 00:53:22.533
Knob, and Guidance.

733
00:53:22.813 --> 00:53:24.903
Canary steps or analysis window:

734
00:53:25.183 --> 00:53:31.989
Smaller steps + longer analysis = safer, slower rollout. maxSurge or maxUnavailable:

735
00:53:32.269 --> 00:53:35.127
Rolling speed versus capacity during the roll.

736
00:53:35.407 --> 00:53:37.157
Probe periods or thresholds:

737
00:53:37.437 --> 00:53:41.959
Fast enough to react, slow enough not to flap; startup covers boot time.

738
00:53:42.239 --> 00:53:49.578
HPA target or stabilization: ~70% util; scale-up fast, scale-down windowed (L3).

739
00:53:49.858 --> 00:53:50.541
Heap:

740
00:53:50.821 --> 00:53:55.066
MaxRAMPercentage within the limit; off-heap headroom (L4).

741
00:53:55.346 --> 00:53:56.562
CPU limits:

742
00:53:56.842 --> 00:54:01.450
Requests yes; hard limits cautiously for latency services (L4).

743
00:54:01.730 --> 00:54:02.711
Image size:

744
00:54:02.991 --> 00:54:06.447
Multi-stage, slim base, faster pulls and scale-ups.

745
00:54:07.797 --> 00:54:08.523
Cost.

746
00:54:08.803 --> 00:54:17.869
Right-size requests or limits: over-requesting wastes reserved capacity (nodes sit idle); under-requesting causes eviction and OOM.

747
00:54:18.149 --> 00:54:21.264
Use VPA recommendations and actual usage.

748
00:54:21.544 --> 00:54:27.475
Autoscaling cost: stabilization and pre-scaling trade a few extra replicas (L3:

749
00:54:27.755 --> 00:54:35.797
8.9, then 11.6 avg) for far fewer breaches, usually worth it on a revenue path, tunable elsewhere.

750
00:54:36.077 --> 00:54:45.485
Spot or preemptible nodes for fault-tolerant, stateless workloads cut compute cost sharply; keep stateful and latency-critical work on on-demand.

751
00:54:46.835 --> 00:54:54.963
Fewer, bigger deploys feel cheaper but cost more in risk and MTTR; small frequent deploys (DORA) are cheaper overall.

752
00:54:56.313 --> 00:54:57.252
Part 7.

753
00:54:57.532 --> 00:54:59.239
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

754
00:55:00.489 --> 00:55:01.129
1.

755
00:55:01.409 --> 00:55:06.806
Recreate deploys for a live service (L1: downtime + 46% errors).

756
00:55:07.086 --> 00:55:09.966
Use rolling at least; canary for anything risky.

757
00:55:10.246 --> 00:55:10.950
2.

758
00:55:11.230 --> 00:55:12.958
No readiness probe (L2:

759
00:55:13.238 --> 00:55:16.033
40% errors routing to a booting instance).

760
00:55:17.383 --> 00:55:26.449
Here is the YAML code. before: traffic the instant the process starts. after: gate routing on real readiness (pool filled, deps reachable).

761
00:55:29.299 --> 00:55:30.046
3.

762
00:55:30.326 --> 00:55:32.523
Liveness probe that checks a dependency.

763
00:55:33.873 --> 00:55:46.887
Here is the YAML code. before: liveness hits the DB, then a DB outage restarts every pod (total outage). after: liveness is cheap and self-contained; readiness handles the DB.

764
00:55:49.737 --> 00:55:50.441
4.

765
00:55:50.721 --> 00:55:52.897
No startup probe for a slow-booting app.

766
00:55:53.177 --> 00:55:57.443
An aggressive liveness timeout kills the JVM mid-boot in a crash loop.

767
00:55:57.723 --> 00:56:00.027
Add a startup probe covering the boot time.

768
00:56:00.307 --> 00:56:01.075
5.

769
00:56:01.355 --> 00:56:04.257
Autoscaling with no stabilization (L3:

770
00:56:04.537 --> 00:56:06.926
827 flapping actions).

771
00:56:07.206 --> 00:56:09.467
Window the scale-down; scale up fast.

772
00:56:09.747 --> 00:56:10.494
6.

773
00:56:10.774 --> 00:56:13.185
Fixed -Xmx in a container (L4:

774
00:56:13.465 --> 00:56:16.707
OOM-kill 137 when the limit changes).

775
00:56:18.057 --> 00:56:29.257
On screen: -Xmx3g in a pod with a 2Gi limit, then SIGKILL. -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75, heap tracks the limit, off-heap headroom.

776
00:56:32.107 --> 00:56:32.854
7.

777
00:56:33.134 --> 00:56:40.558
Hard CPU limits on latency-sensitive services (L4: throttling doubles runtime, spikes p99).

778
00:56:40.838 --> 00:56:44.273
Set requests; be cautious with hard CPU limits there.

779
00:56:44.553 --> 00:56:45.235
8.

780
00:56:45.515 --> 00:56:47.606
Mutable infrastructure or click-ops.

781
00:56:47.886 --> 00:56:51.385
SSH-and-fix creates drift and the untouchable server.

782
00:56:51.665 --> 00:56:54.481
Define infra as code; rebuild, don't patch.

783
00:56:54.761 --> 00:56:57.449
9. latest tags or mutable deploys.

784
00:56:57.729 --> 00:57:00.203
You can't reproduce or roll back to latest.

785
00:57:00.483 --> 00:57:01.827
Deploy by digest.

786
00:57:02.107 --> 00:57:02.811
10.

787
00:57:03.091 --> 00:57:05.609
Config or secrets baked into the image.

788
00:57:05.889 --> 00:57:09.217
Breaks the same image everywhere property and leaks secrets.

789
00:57:09.497 --> 00:57:11.609
Inject at runtime (12-factor).

790
00:57:12.959 --> 00:57:13.791
11.

791
00:57:14.071 --> 00:57:15.329
No rollback plan.

792
00:57:15.609 --> 00:57:19.193
If the old version is gone, a bad deploy is a long outage.

793
00:57:19.473 --> 00:57:25.703
Keep the previous version (blue-green or canary) and use feature flags to decouple release from deploy.

794
00:57:25.983 --> 00:57:26.772
12.

795
00:57:27.052 --> 00:57:28.993
Treating config changes as safe.

796
00:57:29.273 --> 00:57:31.983
A bad ConfigMap breaks prod like bad code.

797
00:57:32.263 --> 00:57:35.569
Version and roll out config with the same care (canary it).

798
00:57:36.919 --> 00:57:37.794
Part 8.

799
00:57:38.074 --> 00:57:39.418
Interview Q and A.

800
00:57:40.668 --> 00:57:41.500
Beginner.

801
00:57:41.780 --> 00:57:42.804
Question 1.

802
00:57:43.084 --> 00:57:46.028
What is a container, and how is it different from a VM?

803
00:57:46.308 --> 00:57:47.673
Thirty-second answer:

804
00:57:47.953 --> 00:57:59.495
A container is an isolated process on the host, using Linux namespaces (its own filesystem or network or PID view) and cgroups (CPU or memory limits).

805
00:57:59.775 --> 00:58:06.729
Unlike a VM, there's no guest OS kernel, containers share the host kernel, so they're light and start in milliseconds.

806
00:58:07.009 --> 00:58:10.316
The image is immutable and runs identically everywhere.

807
00:58:11.666 --> 00:58:12.669
Question 2.

808
00:58:12.949 --> 00:58:16.341
What are readiness and liveness probes, and why keep them separate?

809
00:58:16.621 --> 00:58:17.986
Thirty-second answer:

810
00:58:18.266 --> 00:58:25.285
Readiness answers can I serve right now? on failure the load balancer stops routing to the pod (but doesn't restart it).

811
00:58:25.565 --> 00:58:30.706
Liveness answers am I alive or not deadlocked? on failure the pod is restarted.

812
00:58:30.986 --> 00:58:40.479
They're separate because the responses differ: a starting or dependency-blipped pod should be taken out of rotation, not killed; a hung pod should be killed.

813
00:58:41.829 --> 00:58:42.875
Question 3.

814
00:58:43.155 --> 00:58:45.885
Name the deployment strategies and their trade-offs.

815
00:58:46.165 --> 00:58:47.531
Thirty-second answer:

816
00:58:47.811 --> 00:59:08.355
Recreate (stop all, start all, downtime, full exposure); rolling (replace a few at a time, no downtime, gradual exposure); blue-green (build+test a full new pool, flip, instant rollback, double capacity briefly); canary (send a small % to the new version, watch, promote or roll back, smallest blast radius).

817
00:59:09.705 --> 00:59:10.771
Question 4.

818
00:59:11.051 --> 00:59:13.227
What does horizontal autoscaling do?

819
00:59:13.507 --> 00:59:14.873
Thirty-second answer:

820
00:59:15.153 --> 00:59:22.769
It adds or removes replicas to match load, holding a target metric (like 70% CPU or a custom metric).

821
00:59:23.049 --> 00:59:28.169
The formula is desired = ceil(current times currentMetric or targetMetric).

822
00:59:28.449 --> 00:59:34.891
Scale up fast to meet demand; scale down slowly so a brief dip doesn't tear down capacity you'll need again.

823
00:59:36.241 --> 00:59:37.244
Intermediate.

824
00:59:37.524 --> 00:59:38.633
Question 5.

825
00:59:38.913 --> 00:59:42.881
Design a safe production deployment for a payments-adjacent service.

826
00:59:43.161 --> 00:59:44.527
Thirty-second answer:

827
00:59:44.807 --> 01:00:05.159
Immutable image by digest; deploy as a canary (10%, then 25%, then 50%, then 100%) with automated analysis of error rate, latency and business metrics, and automatic rollback on any SLO breach. readiness or liveness or startup probes gate traffic and restarts.

828
01:00:05.439 --> 01:00:10.964
Keep the previous version for instant rollback, and gate risky behavior behind feature flags.

829
01:00:11.244 --> 01:00:16.449
For schema-touching changes, expand or contract migrations (Ch 28).

830
01:00:17.799 --> 01:00:18.909
Question 6.

831
01:00:19.189 --> 01:00:22.069
How do you run the JVM correctly in a container?

832
01:00:22.349 --> 01:00:23.714
Thirty-second answer:

833
01:00:23.994 --> 01:00:38.522
Size the heap as a percentage of the memory limit (-XX:MaxRAMPercentage), not a fixed -Xmx, and leave headroom for non-heap memory, or the kernel OOM-kills the process (exit 137).

834
01:00:38.802 --> 01:00:52.946
Verify CPU awareness (availableProcessors reflects the quota, sizing GC threads and pools), and be cautious with hard CPU limits on latency services because throttling spikes the p99.

835
01:00:54.296 --> 01:00:55.384
Question 7.

836
01:00:55.664 --> 01:00:59.717
What is a liveness probe that checks the database, and why is it dangerous?

837
01:00:59.997 --> 01:01:01.363
Thirty-second answer:

838
01:01:01.643 --> 01:01:05.589
A liveness endpoint that queries the DB (or other dependencies).

839
01:01:05.869 --> 01:01:19.437
It's dangerous because a dependency outage then makes the liveness probe fail on every pod, so Kubernetes restarts them all, turning a recoverable dependency blip into a full, self-inflicted outage and a restart storm.

840
01:01:19.717 --> 01:01:23.259
Liveness must check only is this process itself responsive.

841
01:01:24.609 --> 01:01:25.633
Question 8.

842
01:01:25.913 --> 01:01:30.265
Explain 12-factor config and why config lives outside the image.

843
01:01:30.545 --> 01:01:31.910
Thirty-second answer:

844
01:01:32.190 --> 01:01:47.785
12-factor says config that varies between environments (URLs, credentials, tuning) lives in the environment, not the code or image, so the same image runs in dev or staging or prod with only injected config differing.

845
01:01:48.065 --> 01:01:50.966
Secrets come from a secrets manager at runtime.

846
01:01:51.246 --> 01:01:56.281
This keeps images immutable and reproducible and prevents secrets leaking into layers.

847
01:01:57.631 --> 01:01:58.697
Question 9.

848
01:01:58.977 --> 01:02:01.580
What is infrastructure as code and why use it?

849
01:02:01.860 --> 01:02:03.225
Thirty-second answer:

850
01:02:03.505 --> 01:02:13.276
Defining infrastructure, networks, clusters, databases, IAM, as versioned, declarative code (Terraform, Pulumi) that you plan (see the diff) and apply.

851
01:02:13.556 --> 01:02:24.457
It makes environments reproducible and identical, changes reviewable and auditable, and kills configuration drift because you rebuild from code instead of hand-editing servers.

852
01:02:25.807 --> 01:02:26.575
Senior.

853
01:02:26.855 --> 01:02:27.901
Question 10.

854
01:02:28.181 --> 01:02:33.578
Design an autoscaling setup for a service with a daily peak and occasional flash sales.

855
01:02:34.928 --> 01:02:36.059
Question 11.

856
01:02:36.339 --> 01:02:41.267
Walk through what happens when a pod is OOM-killed versus throws a Java OOM.

857
01:02:41.547 --> 01:02:42.912
Thirty-second answer:

858
01:02:43.192 --> 01:03:01.389
If the JVM's total memory (heap + metaspace + threads + direct buffers) exceeds the cgroup memory limit, the kernel OOM-killer sends SIGKILL, the process dies instantly with exit 137, no exception, no stack trace, looking like a random crash.

859
01:03:01.669 --> 01:03:13.125
If the JVM's heap is capped below the limit, it throws a Java OutOfMemoryError you can catch, log, and alert on (and with ExitOnOutOfMemoryError, exit cleanly, code 3).

860
01:03:14.475 --> 01:03:15.563
Question 12.

861
01:03:15.843 --> 01:03:17.507
Compare blue-green and canary.

862
01:03:17.787 --> 01:03:19.025
When would you use each?

863
01:03:19.305 --> 01:03:20.670
Thirty-second answer:

864
01:03:20.950 --> 01:03:33.985
Blue-green stands up a complete new environment, tests it out of traffic, and flips all traffic at once, instant cutover and instant rollback (flip back), but double the capacity briefly and a big-bang switch.

865
01:03:34.265 --> 01:03:45.123
Canary sends a small percentage to the new version and ramps up with analysis, smallest blast radius and gradual validation, but needs traffic-splitting and metric analysis.

866
01:03:45.403 --> 01:03:52.913
Use blue-green for risky, hard-to-canary changes (some schema or infra cutovers); canary for most app deploys.

867
01:03:54.263 --> 01:03:55.543
Question 13.

868
01:03:55.823 --> 01:04:00.452
How do you design a CI or CD pipeline that deploys safely and often?

869
01:04:00.732 --> 01:04:02.097
Thirty-second answer:

870
01:04:02.377 --> 01:04:22.089
Build an immutable image (pinned base, tagged by digit or digest), then run tests (unit, integration, contract, a perf gate), then scan (SAST, deps or SBOM, image, secrets), then deploy to staging with smoke or e2e, then canary to prod with automated analysis and rollback, then promote.

871
01:04:22.369 --> 01:04:29.452
Use GitOps so desired state is in Git (deploy = PR, rollback = revert, audit = history).

872
01:04:29.732 --> 01:04:31.033
Track DORA metrics.

873
01:04:32.383 --> 01:04:33.578
Question 14.

874
01:04:33.858 --> 01:04:36.781
Your service is crash-looping right after deploy.

875
01:04:37.061 --> 01:04:38.469
How do you diagnose it?

876
01:04:38.749 --> 01:04:40.114
Thirty-second answer:

877
01:04:40.394 --> 01:04:42.399
Check the pod's exit code and events:

878
01:04:42.679 --> 01:04:58.509
137 = OOM-killed (memory limit versus JVM heap, L4); liveness failures before readiness = a startup probe missing for a slow boot; a readiness endpoint failing = a bad dependency or config.

879
01:04:58.789 --> 01:05:07.813
Look at logs from the crashing container (previous instance's logs), the readiness or liveness config, and whether a config change shipped with the code.

880
01:05:09.163 --> 01:05:10.059
Architect.

881
01:05:10.339 --> 01:05:11.533
Question 15.

882
01:05:11.813 --> 01:05:16.635
Design the deployment platform for 26 services across multiple regions.

883
01:05:17.985 --> 01:05:19.179
Question 16.

884
01:05:19.459 --> 01:05:24.281
How do requests and limits interact with the scheduler, the kernel and the JVM?

885
01:05:24.561 --> 01:05:25.926
Thirty-second answer:

886
01:05:26.206 --> 01:05:35.038
Requests are what the scheduler reserves and bin-packs on (guaranteed capacity); limits are hard caps the kernel enforces via cgroups.

887
01:05:35.318 --> 01:05:39.627
Memory over the limit, then kernel OOM-kill (137).

888
01:05:39.907 --> 01:05:42.169
CPU over the limit, then throttling.

889
01:05:42.449 --> 01:05:57.254
The JVM reads these cgroup limits to size its heap and thread pools, so the JVM flags must be set relative to the limits (MaxRAMPercentage), or the platform and the app disagree, mysterious crashes or throttling.

890
01:05:58.604 --> 01:05:59.948
Question 17.

891
01:06:00.228 --> 01:06:04.281
How do you deploy a change that requires a database schema migration?

892
01:06:04.561 --> 01:06:05.927
Thirty-second answer:

893
01:06:06.207 --> 01:06:10.644
Decouple schema from code with expand or contract (Ch 28):

894
01:06:10.924 --> 01:06:26.412
(1) expand, add the new column or table, backward-compatible, deploy; (2) migrate data and dual-write; (3) switch the app to the new schema (canary the code); (4) contract, remove the old column after the old code is fully gone.

895
01:06:26.692 --> 01:06:33.199
The schema can't be canaried per-pod, so it must be compatible with both the old and new code during the rollout.

896
01:06:34.549 --> 01:06:35.722
Question 18.

897
01:06:36.002 --> 01:06:40.397
Design multi-region deployment and failover for the grocery platform.

898
01:06:41.747 --> 01:06:42.984
Question 19.

899
01:06:43.264 --> 01:06:47.957
What belongs in a 'deployment definition of done' / platform paved road?

900
01:06:48.237 --> 01:06:49.603
Thirty-second answer:

901
01:06:49.883 --> 01:07:13.477
A service isn't production-ready until it has: an immutable image (pinned base, scanned, signed); readiness or liveness or startup probes following the rules; resource requests or limits with a JVM-correct heap; a canary pipeline with automated analysis and rollback; SLOs and dashboards (Ch 24); config or secrets externalized; and infra defined as code.

902
01:07:13.757 --> 01:07:18.216
The platform provides these as a template so every team gets them by default.

903
01:07:19.566 --> 01:07:20.675
Question 20.

904
01:07:20.955 --> 01:07:24.945
How does deployment tie together the rest of the production stack in this book?

905
01:07:25.225 --> 01:07:26.590
Thirty-second answer:

906
01:07:26.870 --> 01:07:34.870
Deployment is where resilience (Ch 18: probes, rollback, shedding during scale-up), observability (Ch 24:

907
01:07:35.150 --> 01:07:52.963
SLO-gated canaries, deploy annotations), performance (Ch 25: warm-up, JVM limits), security (Ch 23: image signing, secrets, least privilege) and reliability (Ch 27: multi-region, DR) all become operational.

908
01:07:53.243 --> 01:07:58.193
A change is only as safe as the pipeline, probes, limits and rollback around it.

909
01:07:59.543 --> 01:08:00.439
Part 9.

910
01:08:00.719 --> 01:08:02.127
Summary and Practice.

911
01:08:03.377 --> 01:08:04.465
Key takeaways.

912
01:08:04.745 --> 01:08:07.411
Ship small, ship often, roll back in seconds.

913
01:08:07.691 --> 01:08:10.785
A deploy you can't reverse quickly is one you shouldn't make.

914
01:08:11.065 --> 01:08:15.545
Canary with automated rollback gives the smallest blast radius (L1:

915
01:08:15.825 --> 01:08:25.915
1.5% versus 46% recreate). readiness gates routing; liveness gates restarts; liveness never checks a dependency (L2).

916
01:08:26.195 --> 01:08:30.291
A startup probe protects a slow JVM boot from liveness kills.

917
01:08:30.571 --> 01:08:35.755
Autoscale with stabilization and warm-up awareness; pre-scale known peaks (L3:

918
01:08:36.035 --> 01:08:40.814
827, then 101 actions, breaches 16, then 1).

919
01:08:42.164 --> 01:08:52.511
Set the JVM heap as a % of the container limit with off-heap headroom, or get OOM-killed (L4: exit 137 versus clean exit 3).

920
01:08:52.791 --> 01:08:59.340
Be cautious with hard CPU limits on latency services, throttling spikes the p99 (L4).

921
01:08:59.620 --> 01:09:05.508
Immutable images by digest, config or secrets injected at runtime (12-factor).

922
01:09:05.788 --> 01:09:12.103
Infrastructure as code + GitOps: reproducible, reviewable, reversible; no click-ops or drift.

923
01:09:13.453 --> 01:09:19.575
Schema changes use expand or contract (Ch 28); you can't canary a shared database.

